<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ragnall ua Ímair</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 921</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Ragnall of York</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ragnall ua Ímair was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> leader, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imar">Ímar</a>, the founder of the Uí Ímair dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>. In 914 his fleet established a base at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a>, from which he raided Munster. He then fought in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, defeating the English and Scots at Corbridge, and around 918 made himself king of York, which he held until his death in 921.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The annals name Ragnall only as ua Ímair, grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imar">Ímar</a>, never by his father's name, so it cannot be known which of Ímar's three known sons, Bárid, Sichfrith or Sitriuc, was his father, if any; he may have descended through a daughter. He was most probably among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> in 902 by Máel Finnia mac Flannacán, overking of Brega, and Cerball mac Muirecáin, overking of Leinster. He seems then to have settled in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> or the Isle of Man, and some scholars call him a King of Mann. The Irish annals first mention him in 914, defeating Bárid mac Oitir in a naval battle off the Isle of Man.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 917 Ragnall and his kinsman Sitric Cáech led fleets to Ireland, Sitric to Cenn Fuait in Leinster and Ragnall to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a>. Niall Glúndub, overking of the Northern Uí Néill, marched south against them, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> won at Mag Femen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-tipperary">County Tipperary</a>, helped by Ragnall's timely reinforcement, and again at the Battle of Confey, where Augaire mac Ailella, overking of Leinster, was killed. Sitric took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> as king. In 918, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a> record, Ragnall left Ireland with Gofraid and the earls Ottir Iarla and Gragabai to fight Constantín mac Áeda, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, at the Battle of Corbridge, after which he made himself king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>.</p><p>His rule there was challenged at once by Christian Vikings opposed to his paganism, who offered their submission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelflaed">Æthelflæd, Lady of the Mercians</a>, until her death in June 918 ended the talks. Three issues of coins were struck while he ruled, perhaps on the orders of Hrotheweard, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>. In about 920 he and the other northern kings came to an agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-elder">Edward the Elder</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> says they chose Edward as father and lord, but many scholars doubt that Ragnall accepted him as overlord.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ragnall died in 921, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a> call him king of the fair foreigners and the dark foreigners. He may already have been dying in 920, when the Irish annals note that Sitric left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> and was replaced there by Gofraid. Sitric succeeded Ragnall as king of the Northumbrians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>. A man the annals call Mac Ragnaill, son of Ragnall, who raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/downpatrick">Downpatrick</a> in 942 and was killed within a week by Matudán, Overking of Ulster, may have been his son.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ragnall ua Ímair” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vikings raided the south-east in the 9th century, and in 914 a large fleet under <b>Ragnall</b> established a base at Waterford, Vedrafjordr.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-waterford">Kingdom of Waterford</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.95,-1.08&amp;year=911" aria-label="York on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4526 312 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Ragnall ua Ímair"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4976 582h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4976 582h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=53.95,-1.08&amp;year=911">What was here in 911 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
